 ## Design decisions
 
-- Cache keys leave the tenant out on purpose: each tenant is pinned to its own worker pool with its own Cache instance (BILL-1874), so it would be redundant.
+- Cache keys always include the tenant id. An earlier version left it out, assuming each tenant was pinned to its own worker pool with its own Cache instance (BILL-1874). That assumption didn't hold in prod, and one tenant saw another's cached results (BILL-2291). Don't rely on process isolation for tenant separation.
diff --git a/src/cache.py b/src/cache.py
index 5205441..38bb8c3 100644
--- a/src/cache.py
+++ b/src/cache.py
@@ -30,6 +30,8 @@ class Cache:
         return len(self._store)
 
 
-def make_key(op, entity_id):
-    # Build the cache key from the operation name and the entity id
-    return f"{op}:{entity_id}"
+def make_key(tenant_id, op, entity_id):
+    # The cache is shared across tenants, so the tenant must be part of the key
+    # (BILL-2291). Entity ids like "c-1" or periods like "2024-05" collide
+    # across tenants.
+    return (tenant_id, op, entity_id)
